Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Introduction",
 FontColor->RGBColor[0.6, 0, 0]]], "Section",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 10}, {5, 5}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.6, 0, 0],
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9841600295676575`*^9, 3.9841600299620647`*^9}, {
  3.984160109139717*^9, 3.9841601650218716`*^9}, {3.9841602725877094`*^9, 
  3.9841603231017017`*^9}, {3.984160401527149*^9, 3.9841604279416504`*^9}, {
  3.984160499972679*^9, 3.984160500582493*^9}, {3.9961034389690857`*^9, 
  3.996103440260908*^9}, {3.9961034702925396`*^9, 3.9961034747700024`*^9}, {
  3.9968395131290913`*^9, 3.996839514274994*^9}, {3.9968788401676693`*^9, 
  3.9968788413149433`*^9}, {3.996880924237612*^9, 3.9968809257483673`*^9}},
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.9803921568627451, 0.984313725490196, 0.9490196078431372],
 CellTags->"intro",ExpressionUUID->"08c73411-79ca-9642-8024-374dd4a41598"],

Cell["\<\
Many mechanical systems are naturally described by Lagrangians whose kinetic \
structure is singular. In such systems, the canonical variables are not all \
independent, and the equations of motion are accompanied by consistency \
conditions that define constraints on phase space. The Faddeev--Jackiw (FJ) \
formalism provides a geometric way of handling these systems: instead of \
introducing constraints through an enlarged Hamiltonian formalism, it follows \
the degeneracy of the presymplectic form and incorporates the resulting \
consistency conditions iteratively.

From a computational point of view, this procedure leads to a sequence of \
enlarged antisymmetric matrices. The structure of these matrices is \
particularly suited to symbolic computation because each reduction step can \
be interpreted as a matrix-bordering operation. BorderedFJReduction \
implements this structure directly in the Wolfram Language, allowing the \
Faddeev--Jackiw reduction to be carried out symbolically while retaining \
exact parameter dependence throughout the calculation.

The mathematical viewpoint is equally important. The kernel of the \
presymplectic matrix identifies the directions along which the Lagrangian \
description is degenerate. Contracting these null directions with the \
gradient of the symplectic potential determines whether the degeneracy \
generates new consistency constraints or persists after the constraints \
already obtained have been imposed. In the latter situation, the reduction \
may signal a candidate for gauge symmetry. The present
implementation deliberately distinguishes this structural identification from \
the subsequent construction and verification of gauge generators.

This notebook illustrates these ideas through explicit mechanical systems. \
The examples are chosen to display different aspects of the reduction: \
noncanonical kinetic terms, coupled mechanical constraints, multiple \
constraints generated in a single bordering step, nonlinear and trigonometric \
constraints, and residual null directions associated with gauge-symmetry \
candidates. Particular attention is given to the symbolic structure returned \
by the paclet, so that the intermediate mathematical objects remain \
inspectable rather than being reduced to numerical output.

The notebook is intended both as a computational companion to the published \
work and as an entry point for experimenting with the Faddeev--Jackiw \
formalism in the Wolfram Language.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.996880902322283*^9, {3.9968811031487865`*^9, 3.9968811162979736`*^9}, 
   3.996920532352169*^9, {3.996920626564966*^9, 3.9969206879687767`*^9}, {
   3.9969208171747284`*^9, 
   3.9969208403325024`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"338da346-7fa1-704c-9872-\
0f56709509b6"]
}, Open  ]],

